WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.680
46,800 Cola palettes moved through one Dutch store and pulled sales off German shells. This is unintended consequences, episode 174. The store was the C-1000 supermarket in Vals. On January 1st, 2003, German shoppers started driving into the Netherlands, Poland, and Belgium for beer, mineral water, and carbonated soft drinks that carried no deposit at all. Environment Minister Jürgen Tritten had just switched the charge on inside Germany.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.507
Tritten, then Federal Environment Minister, had watched reusable containers fall below 72% threshold

00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:41.327
in the 1991 packaging ordinance. Two official surveys confirmed the drop, and the ordinance

00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:46.667
triggered the deposit on its own. He and the ministry saw the fund as a direct pushback toward

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:53.167
refillable glass and polyethylene tariff the late, PET bottles already used by many German breweries

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:58.607
and mineral water firms. The rule also promised to move containers out of residual waste and

00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:05.527
into organized recycling. Roughly 250,000 jobs sat in those refill systems, and Trident argued

00:01:05.527 --> 00:01:11.007
that protecting the jobs meant acting against single-use growth. The deposit was 25 cents

00:01:11.007 --> 00:01:17.567
on containers up to 1.5 liters and 50 cents above that, and only on beer, water, and carbonated

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:22.487
drinks. Proponents expected it to strengthen reuse infrastructure built over the previous

00:01:22.487 --> 00:01:27.547
decade. The ministry was counting on a closed national market, where every purchase and

00:01:27.547 --> 00:01:33.289
every return would follow the same rules. The deposit began nationwide on that first morning,

00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:39.129
even though no single return system existed yet. Early return points were separate island solutions,

00:01:39.489 --> 00:01:44.569
chain by chain, so a can bought in one store could not reliably be redeemed in another.

00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:49.269
Industry groups challenged the rule in court. The Bundis for Fossingsgericht,

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:53.529
Germany's Federal Constitutional Court, upheld it weeks before the start.

00:01:53.529 --> 00:01:58.329
Tritton asked retailers and producers to stop resisting and to build the logistics.

00:01:58.949 --> 00:02:04.469
Within days, customs officers and caught buss recorded a rise in private imports of single-use containers.

00:02:05.029 --> 00:02:10.889
A retailer in Aachen, or Frankfurt, under odor, had to charge the band and later arranged the refund.

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:15.329
A retailer in Vals or Sloobitze had neither charge nor refund.

00:02:16.009 --> 00:02:19.269
The foreign price was already lower before anyone crossed the line.

00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:29.661
Shoppers from as far away as Berlin began regular trips to Sloobice, where a 1.5 liter bottle of lemonade cost about 25 cents, with no fanned.

00:02:30.161 --> 00:02:34.981
One small Polish store reported that German customers routinely bought 10 bottles each.

00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:39.801
Larger supermarket saw sales of deposit-free drinks rise by as much as 70%.

00:02:40.541 --> 00:02:47.041
With returns still fragmented, many people found it simpler to keep buying abroad than to navigate redemption at home.

00:02:47.041 --> 00:02:52.581
Polish and Dutch producers whose one-way containers faced no deposit at home gained

00:02:52.581 --> 00:02:58.101
share against German refillable lines. The European Parliament later noted that Dutch, Belgian

00:02:58.101 --> 00:03:03.801
and Austrian exporters lost nearly their entire German market share. That roughly 1,000

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:09.341
jobs were lost across those countries, and that 5,000 more were at risk. Containers

00:03:09.341 --> 00:03:14.261
bought abroad could not go back through Germany's dual system, so many landed in ordinary

00:03:14.261 --> 00:03:19.481
household waste rather than the yellow bin. Purchasing had moved, and cross-border traffic

00:03:19.481 --> 00:03:24.221
had grown, without an immediate rise in recovery for the containers now being imported.

00:03:24.781 --> 00:03:29.581
Each extra kilometer to the border was a second order effect the ordinance had not modeled.

00:03:30.561 --> 00:03:36.721
By October 2003, a unified return network had begun to appear. Retailers and importers pressed

00:03:36.721 --> 00:03:42.561
for a nationwide clearing system so any retailer could take any container. In April 2004,

00:03:42.561 --> 00:03:47.721
the European Commission formally asked Germany to remove the disproportionate barrier the deposit

00:03:47.721 --> 00:03:53.501
created for imports. Further legal adjustments followed that year. The Commission's intervention

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:58.941
forced equivalent access for foreign one-way packaging, which reduced the border price gap and

00:03:58.941 --> 00:04:04.241
did not erase it. Today, the deposit covers a broader range of single-use containers and runs

00:04:04.241 --> 00:04:10.521
through the centralized Deutsche Fan System GMBH. Cross-border shopping for cheaper non-deposit goods

00:04:10.521 --> 00:04:14.601
remains a noted side effect wherever a neighbor keeps a lower deposit or none.

00:04:15.201 --> 00:04:18.241
The clearinghouse fixed the island solutions inside Germany.

00:04:18.821 --> 00:04:22.181
It left the difference in obligations between member states where it was.

00:04:22.781 --> 00:04:26.821
A charge has to attach somewhere, and this one attached to the German point of sale.

00:04:27.401 --> 00:04:31.901
Any retailer who could sell the same container just outside that jurisdiction was rewarded

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:32.641
for doing so.

00:04:33.221 --> 00:04:38.021
The ministry had set out to measure reusable share, to hold containers and organize recycling,

00:04:38.021 --> 00:04:40.001
and to protect refill jobs.

00:04:40.501 --> 00:04:44.681
What the border household met was a price gap and a price gap rewards the drive.

00:04:45.221 --> 00:04:49.941
An environmental incentive can become a transport incentive when the cheaper legal option sits

00:04:49.941 --> 00:04:51.881
a short distance past an open line.

00:04:52.561 --> 00:04:55.041
The purchase moves, the container type may not.

00:04:55.641 --> 00:05:01.501
Traffic volumes change, that sequence is the one to watch, not as a verdict on 2003, but

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.941
as a design test for rules being written now.

00:05:03.941 --> 00:05:09.181
single-use plastic levees are spreading at uneven speeds, often stopping at a

00:05:09.181 --> 00:05:13.541
national line while the shops on the other side do not charge them. Environmental

00:05:13.541 --> 00:05:17.761
intelligence follows how environmental rules behave once they meet that kind of

00:05:17.761 --> 00:05:22.281
border. How might today's single-use plastic levees account for the same cross-

00:05:22.281 --> 00:05:26.581
border movement before they are introduced? That's the case for today. The

00:05:26.581 --> 00:05:30.221
lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone

00:05:30.221 --> 00:05:33.241
who'd find it useful and subscribe wherever you're listening.

